  <title>Formation Rendezvous</title>

  <para>In the Qt GUI, start with File → New and rename DefaultSC to Sat1 and
  DefaultProp to PropRKV_LowEarth. Clone Sat1 for the other three spacecraft,
  then set each orbit from the table below. Use Edit → New resource for the
  remaining objects. The creation and property forms provide Help for their
  resource type; Apply commits their pending settings. Historical screenshots
  retain the older wx layout and propagator name.</para>

  <section>
    <title>Objective and Overview</title>

    <para>The primary goal of this mission is for you to learn how to create a
    formation flying mission that uses targeting sequences. The mission
    requires spacecraft dependent coordination systems and has plots in
    different spacecraft dependent coordinate systems and targets variables
    that are expressed in spacecraft dependent coordinate systems. Also, this
    mission includes a rendezvous. This tutorial indicates the method in which
    the spacecraft can accomplish formation flying and formation
    rendezvous.</para>

    <para>Download the script file: <link
    xlink:href="files/scripts/FormationRendezvousTutorial.script">FormationRendezvousTutorial.script</link></para>

    <note>
      <title>Reconstructed demonstration</title>
      <para>The legacy script attachment contained an unrelated lunar-transfer
      mission. This replacement follows the four-spacecraft resources, force
      model, transfer times, burn constraints and targeting sequence described
      here. Sat1's initial orbit is recovered from the spacecraft screenshot;
      the source chapter omitted Sat2, Sat3 and Sat4's orbital elements. Their
      small offsets in the table below are explicit reconstruction choices,
      not recovered historical values. Historical screenshots therefore show
      different trajectories and numerical results.</para>
      <para>The first target brings Sat2 to Sat1's position. The second target
      matches their inertial velocities at a later epoch without also
      constraining position. It demonstrates two targeting goals separately;
      it does not establish a simultaneous six-state docking solution.</para>
    </note>

